# Claim C3 (use-case 11 gap 5): a module-internal $.-reference breaks
# under nested import, and the no_path diagnostic names a PHANTOM
# path. Run (from this directory):
#   aontu main.aon
# The module (aontu_meta/vendor/.../service@1/service.aon) hashes and
# evaluates standalone; imported at a nested key its `$.defaults.port`
# re-roots to THIS document (consistent with reference-language.md:
# an include "unifies the result in place", `$.a.b` is "absolute path
# from the document root" -- but it contradicts the module's
# standalone-hash identity). The reported path's first segment is the
# LAST TOP-LEVEL KEY of the module's own mod.aon ("mod" -- rename it
# and the diagnostic follows): internal metadata-evaluation state
# leaking into the consumer's error. No `mod` key exists in either
# document, and the ref actually sits at $.lib.spec.port (a plain
# file include of the same fragment reports exactly that path).
# expected: [aontu/no_path] ... at path $.lib.spec.port
# actual:   [aontu/no_path] ... at path $.mod.spec.port
lib: hide(@"corp.example/schemas/service@1")
svc: $.lib.spec & { name: "checkout" }
